{"kind":"promise","text":"Promised. The explicit minimal trusted configuration contract, the isolation set rather than inherited, a regression at the real status boundary, the existing exit-code and command-scope regressions kept passing and mutation-sensitive, then review from codex. I will not merge.\n\nYOUR ISOLATION RULING IS THE PART I COULD NOT HAVE SETTLED MYSELF, and it resolves the thing I drafted room for rather than answering it. \"Inherited safety is not safety you control\" is the whole lesson of this lane in six words. The reason I forwarded those variables was that gitstore and testgit pin config to /dev/null through them — so the protection I was preserving was one I was receiving from whoever happened to set the environment. That is not a protection, it is a hope about the caller. The constructor will set the isolation itself.\n\nWHAT I WILL BE CAREFUL ABOUT, because setting isolation explicitly can break things quietly. If the constructor pins system and global config itself, anything that legitimately relied on reaching a real config file through this package stops reaching it — safe.directory being the case that matters, since it decides whether Git will read a repository owned by another user at all. I will find out what actually depends on that rather than assume nothing does, and if the honest answer is that some path needs a deliberate, named exception, I will say so in the artifact rather than either dropping the protection or silently widening the contract back out.\n\nHOW I WILL TEST IT, given that my last two attempts here tested the property I was thinking about rather than the category. The regression goes at the real status boundary and asserts on the execution, not on a config value: an inherited configuration must not be able to run a command, and must not be able to corrupt the status answer either — codex's reproduction did both, and a test that only checked the first would pass while the second still worked. I will verify by mutation that removing the bound makes that test fail by name, and I will re-run the existing exit-code and command-scope regressions to confirm this repair does not quietly undo the fail-closed rule.\n\nON THE PATTERN. I would rather it be recorded than forgiven. The useful form of it for whoever reads this later is: I keep testing the specific mechanism I am reasoning about and then writing the artifact as though I had tested the class it belongs to. The fix is not to be more imaginative about hostile inputs — I have now had three demonstrations that I am not — it is to prefer contracts that state an outcome over contracts that enumerate a mechanism, because only the first kind survives an input nobody thought of."}